1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Few industries are as big or as old or as crucial to human survival.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
From the dawn of civilization to the future of a small planet.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, plumbing the arteries of civilization on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,600
If there is one fact of human life we all hold in common,

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,700
it is our dependence on water.

6
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:47,120
Regardless of race, creed, social strata, or historical era,

7
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:49,700
we all rely on it for our very survival.

8
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,960
Through all of its uses in modern daily life,

9
00:00:53,961 --> 00:00:58,520
the average American consumes more than 50 gallons of water per day.

10
00:00:58,521 --> 00:01:06,720
To reach the fixtures that place it at our fingertips, the water is often transported hundreds of miles from its source in the earth.

11
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:16,620
Once it's reached the city, thousands of miles of underground water main carry the water into buildings, where it flows hot and cold at our beck and call.

12
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:21,500
The art and science of plumbing is based on a fundamental law of nature.

13
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:23,180
Water flows downhill.

14
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:28,240
Like nature's rivers, man-made aqueducts are built on a downhill slope.

15
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:31,520
using gravity to propel the water.

16
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,520
The pressure in plumbing pipes is also created by gravity.

17
00:01:36,521 --> 00:01:41,520
Water is pumped to the top of giant towers which are connected to the mains beneath the streets.

18
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:45,520
As long as the tower is higher than the water's final destination,

19
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,520
gravity will maintain pressure and force the water through the pipes.

20
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:52,520
In buildings that are taller than the water tower,

21
00:01:52,521 --> 00:01:56,520
water is pumped to a series of pressure tanks in the building,

22
00:01:56,521 --> 00:02:00,000
each one using gravity to feed the floors below it.

23
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:04,300
After water is used, gravity once again carries it away.

24
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,760
Sewage treatment plants are built at the lowest possible elevation,

25
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:13,280
and sewer pipes are built at an angle so that wastewater can flow downhill.

26
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:18,840
After the sewage has been treated, it flows by gravity into the nearest waterway,

27
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:20,760
where it is reclaimed by the earth.

28
00:02:20,761 --> 00:02:27,520
We've come to expect everything of modern plumbing and rarely do we give it a second thought.

29
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:34,860
But just how crucial it is to our daily lives is made painfully evident by the predicament of developing countries

30
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:37,800
who struggle to survive without its benefits.

31
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:44,280
Indeed, if our plumbing systems were to fail us, our cities and towns would collapse like helpless giants.

32
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:47,920
Plumbing is such a basic, essential need,

33
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:50,900
delivering of fresh, clean, potable water,

34
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,720
water that can be used, and then, more importantly,

35
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:56,340
after it's used, disposed of properly.

36
00:02:56,341 --> 00:02:59,560
Because that sewage, if it's not properly treated,

37
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:02,300
is a tremendous cause of disease.

38
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:04,620
And yet our standards of living,

39
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:06,820
our standards of safety and sanitation,

40
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:09,700
keep that from becoming a reality in our world today.

41
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,140
Today, the plumbing industry is one of the largest in the world.

42
00:03:14,141 --> 00:03:16,000
in terms of business volume.

43
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:21,620
Surprisingly, most of America didn't have what we would consider basic indoor plumbing

44
00:03:21,621 --> 00:03:23,960
until as recently as the turn of the century,

45
00:03:23,961 --> 00:03:27,720
but its principles can be traced back thousands of years.

46
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:37,900
Mesopotamia is what is today Iraq.

47
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:43,220
It was here on the once fertile plain between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers

48
00:03:43,221 --> 00:03:47,960
that Western civilization was born more than 50 centuries ago.

49
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,820
From the earliest days of modern archaeology,

50
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:55,100
the mysteries of our origins have slowly been unveiled.

51
00:03:55,680 --> 00:04:00,160
In the ruins of ancient cities, archaeologists have found unmistakable evidence

52
00:04:00,161 --> 00:04:02,160
that supports a fundamental theory

53
00:04:02,161 --> 00:04:08,500
that the civilization of mankind is inexorably linked to our ability to control the flow of water.

54
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:10,700
What is civilization?

55
00:04:10,701 --> 00:04:17,880
Just about every civilization that we look back on in antiquity that we study and admire

56
00:04:17,881 --> 00:04:24,960
for their contributions to art and literature and science, technology, civil order,

57
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:27,980
also were highly developed plumbers.

58
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:33,680
Fresh water could be carried by hand, but disposing of waste and excess rainwater

59
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:39,200
in early cities required ingenuity, and the earliest civilizations rose to the occasion.

60
00:04:39,201 --> 00:04:46,160
intricate drains were built beneath floors to carry away water the remains of bathrooms and

61
00:04:46,161 --> 00:04:53,360
cesspit similar to modern septic tanks have been found not only in mesopotamia but 1500 miles away

62
00:04:53,361 --> 00:05:00,980
in the indus valley in what is now pakistan and western india here buildings dating back to 2600

63
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:08,300
BC had private wells and bathing areas with drains in the floor the ancient people were as smart as

64
00:05:08,301 --> 00:05:09,001
we are.

65
00:05:09,002 --> 00:05:13,380
They were very intelligent, they were very inventive, and they came up with some

66
00:05:13,381 --> 00:05:15,954
remarkable solutions which we're still using.

67
00:05:15,955 --> 00:05:18,300
They figured out how water runs, and they

68
00:05:18,301 --> 00:05:20,780
figured out most effectively and most efficiently how it runs.

69
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:27,040
By the 6th century BC, the city of Babylon had expanded into a thriving metropolis.

70
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:33,040
For centuries, it had been a center of learning and culture, but under King Nebuchadnezzar

71
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:37,940
II, Babylon grew to become the most magnificent city in the ancient world.

72
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:44,300
The king's bath was most likely a shower poured over him by slaves.

73
00:05:44,301 --> 00:05:50,300
The palace had separate bathrooms with elaborate drains connected to a covered sewer system.

74
00:05:50,301 --> 00:05:57,300
Latrines with raised seats also emptied into the sewer which sloped downward to carry the wastewater away.

75
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:03,300
But the palace's most famous feature were its hanging gardens,

76
00:06:03,301 --> 00:06:10,300
an engineering feat so remarkable that the Greeks named it one of the seven wonders of the world.

77
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:14,300
To irrigate the trees planted atop the seven-meter high wall,

78
00:06:14,301 --> 00:06:21,300
historians believe the Babylonians used a mechanical device called a shadoof.

79
00:06:21,301 --> 00:06:29,300
A shadoof used a fulcrum and wooden beam with a bucket on the end to raise water from rivers to elevated troughs.

80
00:06:29,301 --> 00:06:32,580
I suspect that this is the way that this was done.

81
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:37,100
If you have a whole series of those, three or four of those, you can actually lift water quite high.

82
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:43,000
By 2000 B.C., fresh water was flowing through various forms of pipe.

83
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:48,000
In China, bamboo reeds were hollowed at the joints and used to pipe not only water,

84
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:51,540
but natural gas to and from the ancient salt mines.

85
00:06:51,541 --> 00:06:59,920
On the island of Crete, the Minoan kings had bathrooms with hot and cold running water as early as 1500 BC.

86
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:06,360
In King Minos' palace at Broadnosos, archaeologists have found a large ceramic bathtub,

87
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:09,320
along with the world's first flushing toilet.

88
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,560
The earliest plumbing pipe was made of baked clay and straw,

89
00:07:15,561 --> 00:07:22,440
But the ancient Egyptians, with their advanced metallurgy skills, were among the first to make pipe out of copper.

90
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:28,120
The Egyptians' plumbing prowess was consistent with their expertise as builders.

91
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:34,620
Wells were dug as deep as 300 feet, and the noria, or water wheel, was born in Egypt.

92
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:44,880
Interesting sidelight about the Egyptians is that they may have been the first and perhaps only civilization ever to build bathrooms for the dead.

93
00:07:44,881 --> 00:07:52,760
Their pyramids were kind of temporary resting places for the deceased to pass to the afterlife,

94
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:57,280
and some of the pyramids that have been excavated, it turns out, also had bathrooms in them.

95
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:04,320
The Greeks advanced the art and science of plumbing to an unprecedented level.

96
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:10,160
Hot and cold running water and bathtubs were all part of daily life in ancient Greece.

97
00:08:10,161 --> 00:08:18,440
giant aqueducts carried water into the cities and glorious fountains displayed it but magnificent as

98
00:08:18,441 --> 00:08:23,480
they were the accomplishments of all these civilizations would soon be eclipsed by the

99
00:08:23,481 --> 00:08:31,820
greatest society of plumbers in history the Romans Leonardo da Vinci designed several

100
00:08:31,821 --> 00:08:37,760
toilets with ingenious features like counterweights to close the bathroom door none of them were ever

101
00:08:37,761 --> 00:08:47,280
built modern marvels will be back in a moment we now return to plumbing on modern marvels

102
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:56,560
the california aqueduct built in the 1970s this man-made river is the largest in a system of

103
00:08:56,561 --> 00:09:02,880
aqueducts that supply the los angeles area with water from the north and at 444 miles

104
00:09:02,881 --> 00:09:04,740
It is the longest one in the world.

105
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:13,740
More than a thousand years ago, another great city thrived on water brought in by these marvels of engineering.

106
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:21,940
The magnificent structures that survive to this day have become icons for the brilliance of the Roman Empire, and not surprisingly.

107
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:30,620
They carried millions of gallons of water from the mountains 50 miles away, a feat no civilization had ever attempted before.

108
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:37,100
Once it reached the city, water was distributed through a grid of supply lines under the streets.

109
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,800
What made it all possible was a material that was to give plumbing its name, lead.

110
00:09:42,801 --> 00:09:51,080
The Latin plumus means lead, and the first Roman plumbers were artisans who fashioned sheet lead into everything from basket frames

111
00:09:51,081 --> 00:09:58,760
to the miles of lead pipe that supplied water to the hundreds of fountains, public buildings, and private homes throughout the empire.

112
00:09:58,761 --> 00:10:07,540
But perhaps most importantly, this running water was the lifeblood of one of Rome's most treasured institutions, the baths.

113
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:13,500
I think the most interesting thing about Roman baths is in fact they were a kind of Disneyland for the average Roman.

114
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:22,640
And that was true because of the extraordinary abundance of water in all of these various bath rooms and bathing chambers,

115
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:26,200
but also because they were very elaborately decorated.

116
00:10:26,201 --> 00:10:39,083
A scholar who recently wrote about the Baths of Caracalla has said that this was a means for giving the common people the chance to live the lives of the rich and

117
00:10:39,084 --> 00:10:39,784
famous.

118
00:10:40,580 --> 00:10:48,660
The emperors themselves used these baths as a means of giving the people a kind of wonderful entertainment facility,

119
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:53,380
which would prevent them from thinking of other things like political freedom, for example.

120
00:10:53,381 --> 00:10:58,700
Beneath the splendor of the baths was a marvel of engineering.

121
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:02,900
Millions of gallons of water flowed in and out of the complexes,

122
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:06,960
heated by wood and coal-fired furnaces to precise temperatures,

123
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:09,700
providing an array of choices for the bather.

124
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,820
There were separate steam rooms and radiant heating throughout,

125
00:11:13,821 --> 00:11:17,680
accomplished by pumping hot air through channels beneath the floors.

126
00:11:17,681 --> 00:11:24,460
wastewater drained out of the buildings and into large sewer pipes that emptied into the

127
00:11:24,461 --> 00:11:31,240
Tiber River the largest of these sewers the Cloaca Maxima predated the first aqueduct by a half

128
00:11:31,241 --> 00:11:39,160
century and is still used today as a storm drain in fact its original purpose was just that the

129
00:11:39,161 --> 00:11:44,560
sewer system that ran under Rome was basically to take away the rainwater it was only an afterthought

130
00:11:44,561 --> 00:11:48,160
people thought well as long as that water's going by we might as well throw our stuff into it

131
00:11:48,161 --> 00:11:54,240
and so they did their business into the system and doing their business was often a social event

132
00:11:54,241 --> 00:12:00,800
public latrines had as many as 20 seats arranged in a circle water ran constantly beneath the

133
00:12:00,801 --> 00:12:07,920
seats washing the waste into the nearest sewer when the roman legion set out to conquer the world

134
00:12:07,921 --> 00:12:13,440
they brought their plumbing with them the baths of aquasulas were built by the emperor claudius

135
00:12:13,441 --> 00:12:17,380
in the first century A.D. on the site of a natural hot springs.

136
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:20,140
They were restored in the 16th century,

137
00:12:20,141 --> 00:12:25,700
and today they're a popular tourist attraction in what is now the English town of Bath.

138
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:32,880
Water still flows through the original stone channels into the 80 by 40 foot Great Bath,

139
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:36,400
a kind of scaled down version of the ones back home in Rome.

140
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:42,340
And after nearly 2,000 years, they're a testament to the durability of Roman engineering.

141
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:47,360
As it turns out, the empire itself was not so durable.

142
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:51,740
When it finally fell, so too did the state of the art of plumbing.

143
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:59,360
For once, Rome was conquered, not by a new civilization that surpassed it,

144
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:04,440
but by a bunch of barbarian hordes who were basically nomads and plunderers.

145
00:13:04,441 --> 00:13:08,840
And they didn't really give two hoots about running water.

146
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:13,020
They were never in one place long enough to enjoy indoor plumbing.

147
00:13:17,334 --> 00:13:27,915
Christianity started spreading throughout the Western world and the early Christians were not big fans of bathing and I think one of the reasons is that as I think

148
00:13:27,916 --> 00:13:38,431
everybody knows they were rather poorly treated by the Romans and thus they tended to reject Roman culture and I think they threw out the proverbial baby with the

149
00:13:38,432 --> 00:13:39,132
bathwater.

150
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:52,780
With people crowded into cities during the Middle Ages, the unsanitary conditions brought consequences that were nothing less than deadly.

151
00:13:54,020 --> 00:14:01,400
Throughout what has been aptly named the Dark Ages, entire population suffered some of the worst health consequences in history.

152
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:09,840
Thousands upon thousands lost their lives to waterborne diseases, the nature of which wouldn't be understood for centuries to come.

153
00:14:09,841 --> 00:14:12,920
typhoid, typhus, cholera, dysentery

154
00:14:12,921 --> 00:14:16,660
the resulting condition spawned the infamous Black Plague

155
00:14:16,661 --> 00:14:18,900
which was spread by rats and insects

156
00:14:18,901 --> 00:14:22,520
and killed an estimated one-third of Europe's population

157
00:14:22,521 --> 00:14:26,540
unaware that they were being poisoned by their own filth

158
00:14:26,541 --> 00:14:30,360
people attributed their terrible misfortune to the wrath of God

159
00:14:30,361 --> 00:14:34,280
the traces of plumbing and sanitary practices

160
00:14:34,281 --> 00:14:36,040
that did exist during the Middle Ages

161
00:14:36,041 --> 00:14:39,400
were in monasteries and in the castles of feudal lords

162
00:14:39,401 --> 00:14:42,520
where garterobes served as indoor privies.

163
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:45,080
The garterobes were projection from a building

164
00:14:45,081 --> 00:14:48,360
which inside contained a seat on which you sat

165
00:14:48,361 --> 00:14:52,040
and simply went straight down into the moat or the river or the ground below.

166
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:54,320
And there were several surviving ones in England.

167
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:58,460
And miserable menials would come and scrape away the dirt in the morning

168
00:14:58,461 --> 00:14:59,720
in grand establishments.

169
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:02,540
So the invaders, you know, if your castle was under siege,

170
00:15:02,541 --> 00:15:04,640
everybody would go a lot and you'd see this

171
00:15:04,641 --> 00:15:07,340
and the one thing you didn't want to do was go through that moat.

172
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:19,300
With the Renaissance came a renewed interest in hygiene,

173
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:22,820
and so began the steady march toward indoor plumbing.

174
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:26,820
The first step was to bring water into the home by hand.

175
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,700
Almost every home throughout the land had a wash stand in the bedroom,

176
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:34,920
which, if they were lucky enough, a maid would bring up

177
00:15:34,921 --> 00:15:37,440
with a jug of hot water and a jug of cold water,

178
00:15:37,441 --> 00:15:38,460
and then there'd be the bowls.

179
00:15:38,580 --> 00:15:40,140
And they'd always be there in matching china,

180
00:15:40,280 --> 00:15:42,660
and ravishingly beautiful examples still survive

181
00:15:42,661 --> 00:15:46,020
to this day of these wash handstand sets.

182
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:50,580
Chamber pots of every description made up in form

183
00:15:50,581 --> 00:15:52,020
what they lacked in function.

184
00:15:52,520 --> 00:15:54,480
And emptying them was simple enough.

185
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:57,400
Open the window and toss the contents onto the street.

186
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:01,340
This practice gave rise to the popular nickname

187
00:16:01,341 --> 00:16:03,600
given the modern toilet in Britain.

188
00:16:03,601 --> 00:16:12,440
The proper etiquette for emptying one's chamber pot was to warn passers-by with the French phrase guardé l'eau, meaning look out for the water.

189
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:18,320
This was anglicized to become guardé loup, hence the time-honored term loup.

190
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:26,300
For the origin of the popular nickname John, historians look to none other than the Queen of England.

191
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:32,000
Queen Elizabeth I had a bath once a month, whether she needed it or no.

192
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:34,120
Sean Harrington was her godson,

193
00:16:34,121 --> 00:16:38,360
and he invented apparatus, a machine,

194
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:43,600
a water closet with proper flushing mechanism in 1596.

195
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:47,100
And the Queen also had one made for her,

196
00:16:47,140 --> 00:16:48,400
but she was too frightened to use it.

197
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:49,840
She said it sounded like a thunderstorm

198
00:16:49,841 --> 00:16:53,120
and with these terrifying rushes of water.

199
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:58,940
Ridiculed by his peers, Harrington produced only two of the toilets.

200
00:16:59,700 --> 00:17:03,700
170 years would pass before his invention would resurface.

201
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:12,860
What could be more simple than a tub for bathing?

202
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:17,580
Before indoor plumbing, bathtubs were simply filled and emptied by hand.

203
00:17:17,581 --> 00:17:22,160
It was from this elementary idea that modern baths and showers evolved,

204
00:17:22,340 --> 00:17:25,760
but not without some colorful experiments along the way.

205
00:17:28,940 --> 00:17:34,420
The concept of the shower began in ancient times with waterfalls and buckets of water,

206
00:17:34,421 --> 00:17:39,080
but by the mid-19th century, some intriguing designs were in the works.

207
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:42,800
One of the earliest was the English Regency Shower,

208
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:48,180
which pumped the same water continually from the drain to a cistern over the bather's head.

209
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:55,000
The American Virginia Stool Shower was a seated affair with a hand-operated pump and backbrush.

210
00:17:55,001 --> 00:17:59,360
There were numerous variations including this self-pumping shower

211
00:17:59,361 --> 00:18:02,360
which combined a clean-up with a vigorous work-up.

212
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:07,900
What few may realize is that the newfangled showers of today are nothing new.

213
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:12,280
Their predecessors date back to the mid-1800s.

214
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:16,760
Some historians believe that the fall of the Roman Empire

215
00:18:16,761 --> 00:18:21,920
can be attributed in part to the dementia caused by lead poisoning from the Roman pipes.

216
00:18:22,860 --> 00:18:24,540
Modern Marvels will return.

217
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:35,660
With the first European settlers in New England came the seeds of what was to become the most advanced plumbing industry in the world.

218
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:38,840
But it would take several centuries to get there.

219
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:44,540
Cleanliness was not a treasured value of colonial America by and large.

220
00:18:44,541 --> 00:18:47,919
People rarely bathe. They rarely took baths.

221
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:52,220
They might do so once a month, a couple of times a year.

222
00:18:52,221 --> 00:18:59,220
Nonetheless, as cities were built, running water was a high priority, not for bathing, but for firefighting.

223
00:18:59,221 --> 00:19:04,220
The first water system in America was built in Boston in the mid-1600s.

224
00:19:04,221 --> 00:19:16,220
By 1795, to supply firefighters with water, hollow logs were joined together to create a water main stretching four miles from Jamaica Pond to Fano Hall.

225
00:19:16,221 --> 00:19:17,098
Fannel Hall.

226
00:19:17,099 --> 00:19:22,440
The firemen would simply drill through the walls of these primitive pipes

227
00:19:22,441 --> 00:19:27,420
and plug the hole when they finished. This is the origin of the term fireplug.

228
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:34,600
By the early 1700s, New York City had its own system of wooden mains, but they were

229
00:19:34,601 --> 00:19:38,796
hardly adequate to supply the growing city with water.

230
00:19:38,797 --> 00:19:41,360
In 1835, New York built its first

231
00:19:41,361 --> 00:19:46,583
reservoir in midtown Manhattan, fed by aqueduct from the nearby Croton River.

232
00:19:46,584 --> 00:19:47,940
The reservoir filled

233
00:19:47,941 --> 00:19:53,520
several city blocks and lasted until the turn of the century when two underground water tunnels

234
00:19:53,521 --> 00:19:56,313
that supply New York to this day were built.

235
00:19:56,314 --> 00:19:59,360
Construction on a third tunnel was begun in 1970

236
00:19:59,361 --> 00:20:02,847
and the first channel just recently went into service.

237
00:20:02,848 --> 00:20:05,300
When city water tunnel number three is

238
00:20:05,301 --> 00:20:11,560
completed in the year 2020, it will stretch 60 miles and convey 1 billion gallons of water

239
00:20:11,561 --> 00:20:12,261
per day.

240
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:24,760
During the early years in America, the great outdoors proved an appealing alternative to

241
00:20:24,761 --> 00:20:26,660
the chamber pots from the old country.

242
00:20:27,020 --> 00:20:32,680
But before long, Yankee ingenuity gave birth to an American institution, the outhouse.

243
00:20:32,681 --> 00:20:38,900
Outhouses were basically the bathrooms of the early colonial settlers in this country,

244
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,420
and some of them were quite ornate.

245
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:44,420
The technology was simple enough.

246
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:48,400
A deep hole in the earth, a seat, and a wooden shed with a door.

247
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:53,060
Outhouses continued to proliferate through the 19th century in America,

248
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:56,120
well beyond the advent of the indoor toilet.

249
00:20:56,121 --> 00:21:03,780
with independence came innovation

250
00:21:03,781 --> 00:21:08,620
Thomas Jefferson devised his own waterless indoor privy at Monticello

251
00:21:08,621 --> 00:21:14,480
a chamber pot connected to the outdoors by a series of pulleys operated by servants

252
00:21:14,481 --> 00:21:21,200
meanwhile a new generation of inventors in England had revived John Harrington's bizarre invention

253
00:21:21,201 --> 00:21:25,780
and updated versions of the water closet flooded the British patent offices

254
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,700
American innovators weren't far behind,

255
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,240
but the general public was slow to accept such a radical idea.

256
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:36,900
The early water closets were kind of Rube Goldberg contraptions.

257
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:39,200
They were made of metal.

258
00:21:39,740 --> 00:21:41,840
They were made with various different parts.

259
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:45,140
These parts did not always fit together well.

260
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:46,160
They left gaps.

261
00:21:46,161 --> 00:21:49,700
People enclosed them in wooden cases to make them look like furniture,

262
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:54,100
and waste used to collect in these gaps and ledges and things.

263
00:21:54,101 --> 00:21:59,800
So it was very unsanitary and after a while smelled pretty bad and they didn't work very well.

264
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:05,940
Compounding the problem was the seemingly insurmountable nuisance of sewer gases leaking into homes.

265
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:12,500
The trap seal had already been invented and was in theory a brilliant solution.

266
00:22:13,260 --> 00:22:18,200
A U-shaped section of pipe beneath drains and toilet bowls uses a simple law of physics

267
00:22:18,201 --> 00:22:22,240
to create a water seal blocking sewer gases from entering the home.

268
00:22:23,020 --> 00:22:27,620
But another law of physics had been overlooked, the principle of venting.

269
00:22:28,840 --> 00:22:32,920
What the vent does is allow air to come into the drainage system,

270
00:22:33,100 --> 00:22:38,920
so when that water falls, the air goes with it, and it doesn't suck the water out of that trap.

271
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:40,080
Very, very simple.

272
00:22:40,280 --> 00:22:43,320
If the vent isn't there, it would be like a gallon jug.

273
00:22:43,380 --> 00:22:46,760
When you turn it over, remember that glug, glug, glug that used to happen?

274
00:22:47,180 --> 00:22:48,720
That's what your drains would work like.

275
00:22:49,020 --> 00:22:49,800
Not very well.

276
00:22:49,801 --> 00:22:56,800
Today, venting is a rudimentary part of every plumber's training, but few know how it all began.

277
00:22:56,801 --> 00:23:06,980
There is a record in 1874, a plumber whose name is lost to the ages, unless somebody can recover it somewhere along the way,

278
00:23:07,340 --> 00:23:16,960
who left a diagram for a house that had been having severe problems with odor inside the house that was duplicated for other homes.

279
00:23:16,961 --> 00:23:23,140
and this kind of began the era of what we really can consider to be modern plumbing.

280
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:28,820
Meanwhile, across the Atlantic, the water closet continued to evolve.

281
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:32,940
With improved designs and the increasing availability of running water,

282
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:36,160
the indoor toilet had finally caught on.

283
00:23:37,200 --> 00:23:41,549
But perhaps the most controversial figure in the history of the W.C.

284
00:23:41,550 --> 00:23:43,020
is the most well-known.

285
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:45,560
His name was Thomas Crapper.

286
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:50,020
When this biography of Crapper was published in 1969,

287
00:23:50,640 --> 00:23:52,280
many believed it was a hoax.

288
00:23:52,860 --> 00:23:56,100
What followed was a string of independent investigations

289
00:23:56,101 --> 00:23:58,660
and a heated debate within the industry.

290
00:23:59,300 --> 00:24:02,860
Did Crapper actually invent the forerunner of the modern toilet?

291
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:05,160
Did the man actually exist?

292
00:24:05,620 --> 00:24:10,060
Even today, those who are privy to Crapper's story continue the debate.

293
00:24:10,780 --> 00:24:14,420
He was born in Thorn in Yorkshire, and at the age of 11,

294
00:24:14,421 --> 00:24:22,060
He worked as a plumber, and he got fascinated by the toilets.

295
00:24:22,480 --> 00:24:27,100
And originally, the thing that Crapper developed was called the water waste preventer.

296
00:24:27,240 --> 00:24:33,220
If you lift the lid of your loo now, it's exactly the same as when he developed it 100 years ago.

297
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:39,280
Frederick Humpherson was my great-oncle, and he was apprenticed to Thomas Crapper in 1871.

298
00:24:39,540 --> 00:24:41,900
And these are his apprenticeship papers.

299
00:24:41,901 --> 00:24:43,660
and here's the signatures.

300
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:47,940
Thomas Crapper did not invent a WC pan.

301
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:50,240
He did not invent a WC system.

302
00:24:50,840 --> 00:24:53,520
Indeed, he did not even invent the siphon

303
00:24:53,521 --> 00:24:56,160
for which so much fuss is made.

304
00:24:56,300 --> 00:24:58,520
James Watt didn't invent the steam engine.

305
00:24:58,620 --> 00:24:59,600
He just made it work.

306
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:01,740
Marconi didn't invent wireless.

307
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:03,520
He just made it practical.

308
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:04,660
It's the same with Crapper.

309
00:25:05,440 --> 00:25:07,560
A lot of people were working on it at the time

310
00:25:07,561 --> 00:25:09,100
and he made one that worked.

311
00:25:09,900 --> 00:25:15,500
Americans cannot get over the fact that there was a man selling plumbing fittings with a name like Crapper.

312
00:25:16,260 --> 00:25:18,640
And that's amusing, and it's great, and it's fun.

313
00:25:19,460 --> 00:25:26,280
But that he invented the loo, and he had anything to do with inventing the loo, is, I'm afraid, a myth.

314
00:25:27,040 --> 00:25:34,860
The WC Pan, which is universally recognized today, was designed by Frederick Humpherson in 1884.

315
00:25:34,861 --> 00:25:42,860
That's my great uncle Frederick Humpherson and this is the famous WC pan, the Beaufort,

316
00:25:42,861 --> 00:25:46,860
the first pedestal wash-down closet.

317
00:25:46,861 --> 00:25:53,860
The earliest WCs were encased in wood cabinets, hiding all but the inside of the bowl.

318
00:25:53,861 --> 00:25:58,860
But once they came out of hiding, the era of the designer toilet began.

319
00:25:58,861 --> 00:26:04,540
Little pedestal was decorated in every conceivable fantastical form.

320
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:06,660
I mean, an array of such glories,

321
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:12,440
from the wildest gilding to the wildest shapes, quite wonderful.

322
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:14,180
Two reasons I'd suggest.

323
00:26:14,340 --> 00:26:16,000
One was because of the love of decoration,

324
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:19,760
and the other was to hide the exceedingly immodest object

325
00:26:19,761 --> 00:26:20,840
that they were dealing with.

326
00:26:21,200 --> 00:26:25,460
But I discovered a white swan in the Assize courts in Durham,

327
00:26:25,461 --> 00:26:28,860
where the judges would have had to swing their legs over the back

328
00:26:28,861 --> 00:26:31,920
because the swan's neck came up so they couldn't stand in front of it.

329
00:26:32,300 --> 00:26:33,840
So they would have had to, robes and all,

330
00:26:33,940 --> 00:26:36,020
swing their legs over and sit on this swan.

331
00:26:36,700 --> 00:26:38,960
With the proliferation of water closets

332
00:26:38,961 --> 00:26:42,040
came an unprecedented abundance of sewage.

333
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:46,820
The cities of Europe were the first to pay the unseemly consequences.

334
00:26:47,580 --> 00:26:51,260
London, for example, was choked stiff with filth.

335
00:26:51,340 --> 00:26:53,400
It had three veins, shall we say,

336
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:55,300
three rivers flowing through it, the fleet.

337
00:26:55,460 --> 00:27:03,020
the Thames and the Walbrook stream, and they were all choked stiff with the excrement of the hundreds of privies that dropped into it.

338
00:27:03,021 --> 00:27:12,520
And it became so bad that the streets, too, were filled with excrement, and people were fined if they allowed their privy to overflow.

339
00:27:12,840 --> 00:27:15,640
It was horrible, reeking, stinking, reeking, stinking.

340
00:27:15,641 --> 00:27:21,280
But in 1858, London's newly formed Metropolitan Board of Works

341
00:27:21,281 --> 00:27:23,180
undertook an historic project

342
00:27:23,181 --> 00:27:26,060
under the direction of Sir Joseph Bazalgette,

343
00:27:26,340 --> 00:27:28,960
the construction of London's first sewer system.

344
00:27:30,280 --> 00:27:33,720
Beautiful brick vaulted sewers, like the undercroft of a cathedral.

345
00:27:34,200 --> 00:27:37,260
Under London, throughout, there's a great huge network

346
00:27:37,261 --> 00:27:39,560
of this beautiful Gothic vaulting,

347
00:27:40,080 --> 00:27:42,280
which Sir Joseph Bazalgette built

348
00:27:42,281 --> 00:27:47,940
and thereby saved the population of London and indeed the world

349
00:27:47,941 --> 00:27:50,940
by setting such a good example in the 19th century.

350
00:27:51,940 --> 00:27:54,400
A world away in Chicago,

351
00:27:55,020 --> 00:27:59,100
America's first integrated sewer system was built in 1856.

352
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:02,980
But the young city was facing a contamination problem

353
00:28:02,981 --> 00:28:04,840
that sewers alone couldn't solve.

354
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:09,520
Increasing quantities of raw sewage were dumped into the Chicago River,

355
00:28:09,860 --> 00:28:11,440
which emptied into Lake Michigan,

356
00:28:11,441 --> 00:28:13,880
the city's only source of drinking water.

357
00:28:15,040 --> 00:28:17,680
As more and more people started coming into the city,

358
00:28:18,060 --> 00:28:19,920
they had to take those water intakes

359
00:28:19,921 --> 00:28:22,880
and move them further and further into the lake.

360
00:28:24,140 --> 00:28:26,720
And for a while, it worked.

361
00:28:27,560 --> 00:28:29,660
But then in 1885,

362
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:32,780
there was a tremendous four-day rainstorm

363
00:28:32,781 --> 00:28:35,740
that just flushed all these primitive sewers

364
00:28:35,741 --> 00:28:37,480
way out into the lake

365
00:28:37,481 --> 00:28:39,680
so that the water intake cribs

366
00:28:39,681 --> 00:28:42,940
were inundated with this polluted material.

367
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:47,860
25% of the entire population of the city of Chicago perished

368
00:28:47,861 --> 00:28:50,780
from waterborne diseases during that time,

369
00:28:51,160 --> 00:28:53,920
cholera, typhoid, etc., dysentery.

370
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:57,840
Something like 75,000 deaths.

371
00:28:58,800 --> 00:29:02,260
The Chicago River was the only viable conduit

372
00:29:02,261 --> 00:29:06,280
to carry away the enormous daily flow of sewage from the growing city.

373
00:29:06,940 --> 00:29:10,060
Chicago now faced a nearly insurmountable problem.

374
00:29:10,061 --> 00:29:14,740
Here were these thousands and thousands of people coming to live in the city.

375
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:19,680
They could not live under those conditions where their water supply was polluted,

376
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:26,860
so that a massive, imaginative, dynamic solution had to be found.

377
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:32,020
The answer was a mind-boggling feat of civil engineering.

378
00:29:32,021 --> 00:29:42,100
A 28-mile canal was dug connecting the Chicago to the Des Plaines River, a tributary of the Mississippi, thereby reversing the direction of the river.

379
00:29:42,420 --> 00:29:56,142
It was built with the might and the muscle of 8,500 laborers, most of them newly arrived immigrants from Europe, working with steam shovels and pickaxes and whatever

380
00:29:56,143 --> 00:30:00,300
This canal was dug through solid rock. they could.

381
00:30:00,301 --> 00:30:08,360
it took a great deal of imagination and daring to attempt a feat like this,

382
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:11,260
to actually reverse the flow of a river.

383
00:30:11,960 --> 00:30:14,660
The project was a resounding success.

384
00:30:15,220 --> 00:30:19,820
But it wasn't to be the last civil engineering milestone for the city of Chicago.

385
00:30:20,460 --> 00:30:23,120
Within a few decades, there would be another.

386
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:32,360
A two-story outhouse was built during the 1800s in Crested Butte, Colorado, and still stands today.

387
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:36,160
The upstairs was used when heavy snow blocked the door.

388
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:39,720
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

389
00:30:44,360 --> 00:30:49,220
At the turn of the century, a second industrial revolution was well underway in America.

390
00:30:49,221 --> 00:30:52,780
The late 1800s had seen the development of electricity.

391
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:58,600
the telegraph and telephone and the steel chemical and petroleum industries

392
00:30:58,601 --> 00:31:03,100
America along with Germany had become a world leader in industry

393
00:31:03,101 --> 00:31:07,680
with larger factories powered by electricity and multinational companies

394
00:31:07,681 --> 00:31:10,240
exporting new technologies around the world

395
00:31:10,241 --> 00:31:16,240
in 1913 Henry Ford created the first assembly line for making model T's

396
00:31:16,241 --> 00:31:21,200
and mass production was born by now plumbing in America had caught up with

397
00:31:21,201 --> 00:31:24,791
the rest of the world and the industry flourished.

398
00:31:24,792 --> 00:31:27,880
Bathrooms were designed into new homes as a

399
00:31:27,881 --> 00:31:32,571
standard feature along with hot water heaters and fully plumbed kitchens.

400
00:31:32,572 --> 00:31:33,920
Bathtubs and fixtures

401
00:31:33,921 --> 00:31:39,116
could hardly roll off assembly lines fast enough to satisfy the demand.

402
00:31:39,117 --> 00:31:40,580
As bathrooms started

403
00:31:40,581 --> 00:31:46,400
getting built and bathtubs moved indoors, the tradition of the Saturday night bath kind of

404
00:31:46,401 --> 00:31:53,780
arose around that period and we entered an era in which taking a bath once a week was considered

405
00:31:53,781 --> 00:32:00,240
the civilized thing to do but taking a bath any more than that was considered a waste of time and

406
00:32:00,241 --> 00:32:04,540
probably rather excessive why would you do it more than once a week you were already clean

407
00:32:04,541 --> 00:32:13,320
starting around 1920 an old industry magazine started publishing a campaign for a bath a day

408
00:32:13,321 --> 00:32:18,040
in American society, and they distributed a lot of pamphlets.

409
00:32:18,540 --> 00:32:23,540
Some of our records show over 100,000 pamphlets were distributed to schools

410
00:32:23,541 --> 00:32:25,900
and to plumbing customers throughout the United States,

411
00:32:26,320 --> 00:32:28,000
urging them to take a bath a day.

412
00:32:28,800 --> 00:32:32,500
Plumbing pioneer John Michael Kohler created his first bathtub

413
00:32:32,501 --> 00:32:35,020
from an iron horse trough in 1883.

414
00:32:35,940 --> 00:32:40,780
Then in 1911, the Kohler Company made the first one-piece bathtub with an apron,

415
00:32:40,781 --> 00:32:43,800
which was soon to become the standard bathtub design.

416
00:32:44,700 --> 00:32:49,200
New toilets and wash basins were made of vitreous china with brass fixtures,

417
00:32:49,860 --> 00:32:52,840
and color-coordinated sinks, tubs, and toilets were introduced.

418
00:32:53,860 --> 00:32:57,300
In the 25 years between 1929 and 1954,

419
00:32:58,120 --> 00:33:04,100
manufacturing numbers skyrocketed with a 367% increase in plumbing product sales.

420
00:33:05,420 --> 00:33:08,620
The era of the professional plumber had arrived.

421
00:33:09,620 --> 00:33:13,480
Louis Pasteur's germ theory wasn't widely accepted until 1890,

422
00:33:13,960 --> 00:33:17,420
but now the nature of waterborne disease was well understood.

423
00:33:18,300 --> 00:33:22,680
Finally, the world could see just how high the stakes of sanitary plumbing were.

424
00:33:24,460 --> 00:33:29,100
Professional associations were formed and formal apprenticeship programs were put in place.

425
00:33:29,300 --> 00:33:33,220
By the mid-30s, uniform plumbing codes were legislated

426
00:33:33,221 --> 00:33:36,780
and manufacturers established standards for plumbing products.

427
00:33:36,781 --> 00:33:45,800
Chicago, 1930

428
00:33:45,801 --> 00:33:52,580
The sanitary ship canal had successfully reversed the flow of the Chicago River away from Lake Michigan

429
00:33:52,581 --> 00:33:57,560
and was hailed internationally as one of the seven wonders of American engineering

430
00:33:57,561 --> 00:34:03,820
But with the population continuing to grow, the city's problems were far from over

431
00:34:03,821 --> 00:34:17,821
At that time, through the canal that they had built in the North Shore Channel, the city was taking 12,000 cubic feet of water per second out of Lake Michigan to flush

432
00:34:18,233 --> 00:34:19,700
the system clean.

433
00:34:20,320 --> 00:34:31,447
But even with that large amount of water, it was still more or less a large open sewer, particularly whenever it rained, we would have a fresh influx of pollution into

434
00:34:31,448 --> 00:34:32,580
the river itself.

435
00:34:32,581 --> 00:34:40,360
To address the problem, the Chicago Sanitary District created the largest and most advanced sewage treatment plant ever built.

436
00:34:41,040 --> 00:34:47,880
The facility, built on a 570-acre plot in nearby Stickney, was decidedly ahead of its time.

437
00:34:49,140 --> 00:34:56,980
When the EPA set strict new standards for sewage treatment more than 30 years later, the Stickney plant already exceeded them.

438
00:34:57,980 --> 00:35:03,660
By 1939, the Stickney plant was processing 1.2 billion gallons per day of wastewater,

439
00:35:04,260 --> 00:35:06,960
making it the largest facility of its kind in the world.

440
00:35:06,961 --> 00:35:12,500
But even that couldn't solve a problem that continued to plague the Chicago Sanitary District.

441
00:35:13,480 --> 00:35:18,260
Like all older cities, Chicago uses a combined sewer system

442
00:35:18,261 --> 00:35:22,240
for both sanitary waste and rainwater collected in storm drains.

443
00:35:22,700 --> 00:35:25,300
The problem? Intense rainstorms.

444
00:35:25,301 --> 00:35:29,000
On the average, the Windy City has 100 of them a year.

445
00:35:30,200 --> 00:35:32,620
Once you get more than a little bit of rain,

446
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:37,580
it was necessary to divert this mixture of sewage and rainwater

447
00:35:37,581 --> 00:35:41,740
away from the sewer pipe because it would flood out the sewage treatment plant.

448
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:48,540
And so at various points along the Chicago River and other waterways, the canals,

449
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:50,660
there would be overflow points.

450
00:35:50,661 --> 00:35:55,200
And so the mixture of polluted material, rainwater and sewage,

451
00:35:55,300 --> 00:35:57,500
would then go into that waterway.

452
00:35:58,340 --> 00:36:02,900
At times, the overflow would back up into the basements of thousands of homes,

453
00:36:03,060 --> 00:36:05,440
and scores of highway underpasses would be flooded,

454
00:36:05,860 --> 00:36:07,580
grinding traffic to a halt.

455
00:36:09,580 --> 00:36:14,200
Once again, Chicago met the challenge with a bold and ingenious solution.

456
00:36:14,840 --> 00:36:17,320
Using the latest tunnel boring technology,

457
00:36:17,820 --> 00:36:21,560
engineers dug a series of tunnels through the thick dolomite rock

458
00:36:21,561 --> 00:36:25,120
between 150 and 300 feet below the city,

459
00:36:25,300 --> 00:36:28,300
to literally bottle the rainstorms.

460
00:36:30,300 --> 00:36:35,300
The drop shaft would go down to where the tunnel is,

461
00:36:35,301 --> 00:36:38,300
in some cases dropping down 300 feet.

462
00:36:38,301 --> 00:36:44,300
The tunnels themselves are as large as 35 feet in diameter

463
00:36:44,301 --> 00:36:51,300
and can contain up to a billion gallons of combined sewage mixture.

464
00:36:51,301 --> 00:37:01,300
The water is stored in the tunnels and in reservoirs until the storm is over, then it's pumped back to the surface and into the sewage treatment plant.

465
00:37:05,300 --> 00:37:08,300
World War II had a powerful impact on the plumbing industry.

466
00:37:08,301 --> 00:37:15,740
industry by 1940 the war production board had severely restricted the use of iron steel and

467
00:37:15,741 --> 00:37:21,860
copper and manufacturers had to experiment with new materials such as cast iron and plastics

468
00:37:21,861 --> 00:37:28,280
the innovation started a new wave of improved plumbing products that has continued ever since

469
00:37:28,281 --> 00:37:35,720
it was as recently as the 1940s that Al Mohan invented a faucet most of us take for granted

470
00:37:36,640 --> 00:37:40,220
Al Mohn figured out a way to do it with just one handle,

471
00:37:40,380 --> 00:37:44,220
and by manipulating this handle, achieved the right mixture of hot and cold water,

472
00:37:44,380 --> 00:37:48,140
and now single-handed faucets are among the most popular around.

473
00:37:48,860 --> 00:37:52,220
Modern hot water heaters are all equipped with an essential device

474
00:37:52,221 --> 00:37:55,020
invented in the 30s by the Watts Regulator Company,

475
00:37:55,021 --> 00:37:57,160
who still manufactures them today.

476
00:37:57,640 --> 00:38:00,200
It's known in the trade as a T&P valve.

477
00:38:01,600 --> 00:38:04,460
Without a temperature and pressure relief valve,

478
00:38:04,461 --> 00:38:11,360
The pressures inside of a water heater can build up to the extent that your water heater may someday take off.

479
00:38:11,460 --> 00:38:19,812
And if you live in a three-story house, it could conceivably go through the first floor and the second floor and the third floor and the roof and end up in a

480
00:38:19,813 --> 00:38:22,100
neighbor's house maybe a quarter mile away.

481
00:38:24,040 --> 00:38:26,960
These things used to happen quite commonly in America.

482
00:38:27,180 --> 00:38:32,580
They still happen once in a while in homes where the water heater does not have a T&P valve.

483
00:38:32,581 --> 00:38:37,420
But this was a great invention, and I'm sure it saved untold lives in America,

484
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:40,280
and most people don't even know they had them.

485
00:38:40,940 --> 00:38:42,320
And what will they think of next?

486
00:38:43,420 --> 00:38:47,540
Computerized toilets and remote-control whirlpools are only the beginning.

487
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:53,820
Cutting-edge science is doing things with wastewater that even the Romans could never have dreamed possible.

488
00:38:56,420 --> 00:39:00,440
Harry Truman upgraded the White House plumbing in 1948.

489
00:39:00,441 --> 00:39:07,940
but until 1902 the mansion had no indoor plumbing at all modern marvels will return

490
00:39:07,941 --> 00:39:17,300
the basics of safe sanitary plumbing are no longer a challenge for the modern world but

491
00:39:17,301 --> 00:39:26,680
do the innovations continue in a word yes the bathroom market has exploded with new materials

492
00:39:26,681 --> 00:39:30,920
of all kinds, new designs, new shapes from a multitude of manufacturers.

493
00:39:31,880 --> 00:39:36,480
This new toilet from Japan has a computer in it to control the flow of water.

494
00:39:37,160 --> 00:39:41,180
It also has a heated seat, a built-in fan to remove odors,

495
00:39:41,181 --> 00:39:46,360
and a remote-controlled washlet, which adds the function of a bidet to the unit.

496
00:39:47,920 --> 00:39:51,720
High-tech bathrooms, computerized temperature controls,

497
00:39:51,721 --> 00:39:54,720
multi-function self-cleaning shower heads,

498
00:39:55,320 --> 00:40:02,200
and waterproof wireless remotes for whirlpools that control everything from lighting to the flow of the jets.

499
00:40:03,020 --> 00:40:08,440
But beyond the high-tech features, there is a theme to the new wave of plumbing products,

500
00:40:08,860 --> 00:40:15,820
and it's consistent with the latest preoccupation of municipalities around the world, water conservation.

501
00:40:15,821 --> 00:40:23,020
Somebody such as myself who may be discussing plumbing history 25, 30, 40, 50 years from now

502
00:40:23,021 --> 00:40:29,760
may look back and define one of the watersheds of plumbing history

503
00:40:29,761 --> 00:40:33,600
as the 1992 Plumbing Products Efficiency Act,

504
00:40:33,601 --> 00:40:42,480
which involved the federal government mandating that no toilet will flush with more than 1.6 gallons of water.

505
00:40:42,920 --> 00:40:45,180
The previous standard was 3.5 gallons.

506
00:40:45,181 --> 00:40:50,040
They also put restrictions on flows of faucets and showerheads as well.

507
00:40:50,380 --> 00:41:00,320
I think it's the first step of what is going to be a continuing trend towards making plumbing products more environmentally friendly.

508
00:41:00,840 --> 00:41:04,620
The future is going to be required that we become custodians of water.

509
00:41:05,300 --> 00:41:10,120
We deliver to you fresh, potable, safe water in basically unlimited quantities.

510
00:41:10,500 --> 00:41:12,460
And then we take that waste and we send it away.

511
00:41:13,020 --> 00:41:15,100
Our challenge is going to be to come to you

512
00:41:15,101 --> 00:41:18,720
and to continue doing what we've been doing for the last 10 years,

513
00:41:18,721 --> 00:41:23,020
which is slowly reducing the level of water consumption,

514
00:41:23,400 --> 00:41:25,740
because it's going to be a battle that we're all facing.

515
00:41:31,620 --> 00:41:33,020
Santee, California.

516
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:39,200
This community of 54,000 was built in a desert valley just east of San Diego.

517
00:41:39,680 --> 00:41:42,200
There's virtually no rainfall here.

518
00:41:42,460 --> 00:41:47,180
and Santee is at the end of the pipeline that supplies water to Southern California.

519
00:41:48,240 --> 00:41:51,460
But despite the short supply of fresh water and droughts,

520
00:41:51,700 --> 00:41:54,260
this town is teeming with lush greenery,

521
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:57,620
and not one but seven recreational lakes.

522
00:41:58,300 --> 00:42:01,920
It's a phenomenon that would have been impossible a hundred years ago.

523
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:04,620
The secret? Recycled water.

524
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:08,180
At this state-of-the-art treatment facility,

525
00:42:08,181 --> 00:42:14,880
wastewater from Santee sewers is converted to crystal clear water so clean that it meets

526
00:42:14,881 --> 00:42:17,442
laboratory standards for drinking water.

527
00:42:17,443 --> 00:42:20,900
Once the chemically activated sludge has been removed,

528
00:42:21,060 --> 00:42:26,420
the water is filtered and put through an advanced process of biological and chemical treatment.

529
00:42:27,480 --> 00:42:31,727
A million gallons of reclaimed wastewater are fed to the lakes each day.

530
00:42:31,728 --> 00:42:33,320
Another million gallons are

531
00:42:33,321 --> 00:42:35,136
used to irrigate the community.

532
00:42:35,137 --> 00:42:38,240
The water is distributed throughout Santee through an

533
00:42:38,241 --> 00:42:43,700
underground pipeline system that's carefully labeled and color-coded in purple, the standard

534
00:42:43,701 --> 00:42:45,852
color to indicate reclaimed water.

535
00:42:45,853 --> 00:42:49,840
During the three decades since Santee launched its breakthrough

536
00:42:49,841 --> 00:42:54,620
experiment, dozens of other communities around the country have implemented their own reclaimed

537
00:42:54,621 --> 00:42:57,204
water programs. They can treat any kind of water.

538
00:42:57,205 --> 00:42:59,260
The dirtier it is that the water costs,

539
00:42:59,261 --> 00:43:00,020
but you can treat it.

540
00:43:00,800 --> 00:43:06,120
And then they've also established processing treatments for water treatment

541
00:43:06,121 --> 00:43:09,820
so that literally you could take this sewage water very shortly

542
00:43:09,821 --> 00:43:11,720
and use it for drinking water.

543
00:43:12,660 --> 00:43:14,120
Shocking as it sounds,

544
00:43:14,640 --> 00:43:18,080
experiments are currently underway for that very process.

545
00:43:18,081 --> 00:43:22,680
The deliberate and safe introduction of recycled wastewater

546
00:43:22,681 --> 00:43:25,220
into potable water supplies.

547
00:43:26,080 --> 00:43:31,000
The importance of keeping wastewater as far as possible from drinking water

548
00:43:31,001 --> 00:43:35,220
is one of plumbing history's most important and expensive lessons.

549
00:43:36,140 --> 00:43:39,520
Now, modern technology has brought us full circle.

550
00:43:40,300 --> 00:43:43,500
If mankind has walked the earth for roughly two million years,

551
00:43:43,501 --> 00:43:46,520
it's only during the last ten thousandth of that time

552
00:43:46,521 --> 00:43:49,720
that we've had what we consider basic modern plumbing.

553
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:53,740
And yet, living without it is all but unfathomable,

554
00:43:54,300 --> 00:43:57,680
Mundane as it may seem, plumbing is our past.

555
00:43:58,020 --> 00:43:59,040
It is our future.

556
00:43:59,780 --> 00:44:03,020
It is the arteries of our civilization.

557
00:44:06,380 --> 00:44:10,220
Imagine going to work knowing that you have a 50% chance of surviving.

558
00:44:10,520 --> 00:44:13,360
Well, that's the way it was for electrical linesmen a century ago.

559
00:44:13,361 --> 00:44:19,360
But now, how do today's electricians handle half a million volts of electricity without becoming seriously sizzled?

560
00:44:19,361 --> 00:44:25,480
You'll be amazed to discover how these guys deal with high voltage on modern marvels, next on the History Channel.

